PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2}

I see the appeal of B1 from the detailed sequences shared by several colleagues, but I'm still concerned it overstates the forcing power and underestimates Black's defensive options. Playing B1 puts C1-C2 in atari, yes, but Black's immediate B2 both saves those stones and connects the entire group into one shape with liberties at A1/A3/A4. From there, White's B1 stone is reduced to a single liberty at A1; any follow-up attempt at A3 or A4 risks leaving White vulnerable to capture or allowing Black to resolve the corner without self-atari. This feels like it hands Black an easy connection rather than a true kill.

By contrast, B2 strikes me as the real vital point because it directly severs the potential link between A2, the B3-B4 stones, and the C1-C2 group while threatening immediate capture via the surrounding White wall at C3/C4. Black has no safe reply that preserves two eyes—B1 captures the invading stone but leaves the remaining shape short on liberties and eye space along the A-file, and any attempt to extend outward (A3/A4) can be met with continued pressure that exploits the damezumari. The sequences proposed for B1 seem to assume Black must respond passively, but the eye-space rules in this bent-three configuration suggest B2 creates more unavoidable pressure from the start. If anyone has a concrete line showing why B2 fails against best play, I'd like to see it, as the current arguments for B1 still leave room for Black to live.

