After reviewing the detailed sequences for B1 in round 1, I still see a critical gap that prevents it from being a true forcing kill. The damezumari line assumes Black is locked into passive responses at every step, but after 1. B1 B2 2. A4 A1 3. A3, Black's 7-stone group does retain one internal point at B1. While suicide rules block Black from playing there immediately, the capture of the B1 stone at move 3... A1 also resets the local liberty structure around A1-A2, allowing Black a later opportunity to extend or sacrifice a single stone (such as C1) to create an eye at A1 or A3 before White can seal with B1. This isn't a full two-eye shape, but it does give Black enough breathing room to avoid outright capture on the next turn, especially with the outside White wall at B5/C4/C3 not fully connected in a way that prevents all escape routes. 

By contrast, any attempt to start at B2 hands Black an immediate capture at B1 and two solid eyes, so it fails outright. The net result is that no single first move by White creates an unavoidable kill sequence against best play; Black can always stabilize or capture the invading stone. This leaves us with no viable initiating move.

